<div class="editorial-layout"><div class="chapter-list"><details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-1" open><summary><span class="chapter-count">01</span><h3>The plan for your space</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Aurora asks more of a landscape than most Denver suburbs. It sits at the edge of an area sometimes called Hail Alley, where sudden summer hailstorms are common enough that plant selection has to account for them, not just for sun and drought. Add the city’s own push toward water-wise design, and Aurora has become one of the more demanding, and more interesting, places in the metro to design a drought-tolerant yard.</p>

<p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design has spent over a decade designing landscapes across this region, and Aurora’s specific mix of climate pressures, low water, high sun, occasional hail, shapes how we approach every project here.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-2"><summary><span class="chapter-count">02</span><h3>What Makes Landscaping “Drought-Tolerant”?</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Drought-tolerant landscaping means designing a yard around plants that survive and thrive with minimal supplemental water once established, paired with irrigation and soil practices that make the most of every drop. In Colorado, this approach is often called xeriscaping, a term Denver Water coined back in 1981. The two terms describe essentially the same idea, just with slightly different wording.</p>

<p>A drought-tolerant yard is not a bare, rocky yard. It’s fully planted, often with native grasses, perennials, and shrubs that happen to need far less water than Kentucky bluegrass, arranged to give you color through more than one season.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-3"><summary><span class="chapter-count">03</span><h3>Why Aurora Has Pushed Harder on This Than Most Cities</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Aurora runs its own Grass Replacement Incentive Program, known locally as GRIP, aimed at helping homeowners swap out high-water lawns for drought-tolerant alternatives. The city has also put rules in place limiting purely decorative turf on new single-family construction, which means a growing share of new Aurora homes are required to think about drought-tolerant landscaping from day one, not as an optional upgrade later.</p>

<p>Then there’s the weather itself. Aurora’s exposure to hailstorms means plant choices that hold up structurally, not just plants that survive on less water, matter here in a way they don’t in every part of the metro.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-4"><summary><span class="chapter-count">04</span><h3>Our Drought-Tolerant Landscaping and Xeriscaping Services in Aurora</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><h3><strong>Design Built for Aurora’s Conditions</strong></h3>

<p>We design around your yard’s sun exposure and drainage, and we factor in plant resilience to wind and hail, not just drought tolerance alone. Some ornamental grasses and perennials handle a hailstorm far better than broad-leafed plants that shred easily.</p>

<h3><strong>New Construction Compliance</strong></h3>

<p>If you’re building new in Aurora, we can design a front or side yard that meets the city’s current turf limitations while still looking intentional rather than like a compliance afterthought.</p>

<h3><strong>Lawn-to-Xeriscape Conversion</strong></h3>

<p>For existing homes, we remove the old turf, correct the soil where needed, and rebuild around drip irrigation and a drought-tolerant plant palette suited to Aurora specifically.</p>

<h3><strong>Installation and Maintenance</strong></h3>

<p>Grading, irrigation, hardscaping, and planting are handled directly by our team, typically led by Francisco Garcia and lead designer Chris. We follow up with maintenance during the first year or two while plants establish.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-5"><summary><span class="chapter-count">05</span><h3>Is Your Aurora Yard a Fit for Drought-Tolerant Landscaping?</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><ul>
<li>You’re building new and need a front or side yard that meets Aurora’s turf rules</li>
<li>Your lawn browns out every summer no matter your watering schedule</li>
<li>You’ve had plants shredded or knocked back by hail and want something more resilient next time</li>
<li>You live in a newer neighborhood like Southlands or Tallyn’s Reach with strict HOA landscaping guidelines</li>
<li>You want to spend less time and money keeping grass alive through a hot Aurora summer</li>
</ul>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-6"><summary><span class="chapter-count">06</span><h3>Water-Wise Landscaping Support in Aurora</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Aurora Water’s GRIP program is designed to help homeowners offset part of the cost of replacing high-water turf with a drought-tolerant landscape. Because funding levels and eligibility details change from year to year, we won’t list specific figures here. The program has been running for several years, and our team can help point you toward current details as part of your estimate.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-7"><summary><span class="chapter-count">07</span><h3>Why Aurora Homeowners Choose Ridgewell for Drought-Tolerant Landscaping</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design was founded in 2011 by longtime Colorado resident Francisco Garcia, and more than a decade of hands-on experience across this climate shapes every design we build. We’re licensed and insured in Colorado, hold a perfect 5.0-star rating on Google, and keep our team small enough that Francisco and Chris are personally involved in most Aurora projects from start to finish.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-8"><summary><span class="chapter-count">08</span><h3>Get Your Free Drought-Tolerant Landscaping Estimate in Aurora</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Whether you’re building new or replacing a struggling lawn, let’s design something that can actually handle Aurora’s weather.<a href="/contact/"> Get Your Free Estimate</a> and our team will follow up.</p>

<hr/></div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-9"><summary><span class="chapter-count">09</span><h3>Your questions, answered</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><h3><strong>Does Aurora require drought-tolerant landscaping for new homes?</strong> </h3>

<p>Aurora has rules limiting purely decorative turf on new single-family construction, which means many new builds need a front or side yard designed around drought-tolerant plants rather than traditional grass. Requirements can vary by development, so it’s worth checking with your builder or the city for your specific lot.</p>

<h3><strong>What is Aurora’s GRIP program?</strong> </h3>

<p>GRIP stands for Grass Replacement Incentive Program, run by Aurora Water to help homeowners offset part of the cost of converting high-water turf to a drought-tolerant landscape. Program terms change periodically, so we recommend confirming current details, and we can point you to the latest information during your estimate.</p>

<h3><strong>Will drought-tolerant plants survive Aurora’s hailstorms?</strong> </h3>

<p>Some hold up much better than others. Ornamental grasses and many native perennials tend to recover well from hail damage, while broad, tender leaves can shred badly. Plant selection with Aurora’s storm patterns in mind makes a real difference over the life of the landscape.</p>

<h3><strong>How is drought-tolerant landscaping different from xeriscaping?</strong> </h3>

<p>They describe the same core idea. Xeriscaping is the specific term coined in Colorado in 1981, while drought-tolerant landscaping is a more general description of the same water-saving approach. You’ll see both terms used interchangeably.</p>

<h3><strong>Can I keep some grass and still go drought-tolerant?</strong></h3>

<p>Yes. Many homeowners keep a small, functional patch of turf for kids or pets and convert the rest of the yard, rather than removing every blade of grass. It’s a common middle-ground approach.</p>

<h3><strong>How much water does drought-tolerant landscaping save in Aurora?</strong> </h3>

<p>Once established, drought-tolerant yards typically use significantly less outdoor water than a traditional lawn, with many homeowners and water providers reporting reductions of 50 percent or more.</p>
</div>
</details>
</div>
